  "brief_md": "# Evidence Brief: A08 — Wiki-style Cycling Hazard Map\n\n## Idea\nA wiki-style cycling route map where local cyclists flag and correct dangerous route segments, for urban commuter cyclists, possibly advocacy groups or municipalities.\n\n## Payer\nNo clean single payer found. The closest real-world analog, BikeMaps.org, is funded by government research grants ($930,000 from Canada's Public Health Agency), not by end users. [https://gobiking.ca/2016/12/13/%e2%97%8f-is-the-bikemaps-org-project-helping-to-get-more-people-on-their-bikes/] The closest consumer-paid analog, Pointz, uses a freemium subscription ('Pointz Plus') but needed $520,000 in outside VC/accelerator funding to get there and does not publish its subscription price. [https://zagdaily.com/people/we-rank-every-single-road-in-the-us-on-a-bike-safety-scale/]\n\n## Competitors\n- **BikeMaps.org** — free, grant-funded research tool, active across 9+ Canadian cities and worldwide. [https://bikemaps.org/]\n- **Pointz** — freemium bike-safety mapping app, $520k raised, Forbes 30U30 founders, premium tier with roadside assistance. [https://apps.apple.com/us/app/pointz-plan-safer-on-bikes/id1580218237]\n- **LaneSpotter** — free, crowdsourced 'Waze for cycling,' monetization via sponsorship/ads (not user fees); launched 2017, reached 11 cities. [https://www.bicycling.com/news/a20032353/lanespotter-app-maps-best-bike-routes/]\n- **WABA Crash Tracker** — free hazard/near-miss/crash reporting tool run by a regional nonprofit advocacy org (adjacent, but functionally overlapping). [https://waba.org/crashtracker/]\n- **Davis Bike Hazard Map** — free, open-source, volunteer hobby project in private beta. [https://github.com/ChelseaKR/davis-bike-hazard-map]\n\nAll named direct competitors are free to the end user; none has a validated consumer-paid pricing model.\n\n## Acquisition Channel\n- Paid social app-install ads: industry benchmark CPI ~$1–$5 per install. [https://www.wask.co/facebook-advertising-costs/cpi-cost-per-install]\n- Partnership with existing bike advocacy nonprofits (who already run similar hazard-report tools and have dues-paying members, e.g., League of American Bicyclists at $50/yr) as a lower-cost, higher-trust distribution channel. [https://bikeleague.org/join/]\n\n## Demand Evidence\n- Seed quote: \"A city map for cyclists that is updated and reviewed by other cyclists.\" [https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=45500937]\n- \"If you are here to report ... a dangerous road, your report will help us advocate for lasting change for the road.\" — WABA crash reporting tool copy, reflecting real demand for this exact function. [https://waba.org/crashtracker/]\n- \"Why can't we rely on other cyclists for help safely navigating the city?\" — LaneSpotter founder describing the origin of user pain. [https://www.bicycling.com/news/a20032353/lanespotter-app-maps-best-bike-routes/]\n\n## Case For\nA solo operator with AI coding agents could plausibly ship a thin MVP (map + pin/flag/edit UI + simple moderation) within six weeks — the mechanic closely resembles well-trodden OpenStreetMap-style crowdsourced editing. The category has nearly a decade of proof that cyclists and advocacy groups want this function (BikeMaps.org, LaneSpotter, Pointz, WABA, Davis project), and there's a credible non-consumer payer path (municipal Vision Zero programs, research grants) that doesn't require viral consumer adoption.\n\n## Case Against\nEvery direct, named competitor is free — funded by grants, VC, or nonprofit membership dues — and none has proven consumers will pay directly. The product that got closest to monetization (Pointz) took years and $520k to build trust, routing data, and partnerships; a solo six-week build has no seed data and no existing relationship with the advocacy orgs or municipalities who already run equivalent free tools. Liability and moderation (false/malicious hazard flags affecting rider safety or property/infrastructure decisions) are ongoing costs that don't shrink for a one-person shop, and the market shows a strong gravitational pull toward \"free, nonprofit or publicly funded\" rather than \"paid startup.\"\n\n## Open Questions\n1. Would cities or advocacy nonprofits pay for or integrate a third-party tool instead of maintaining their own free forms?\n2. Can one operator seed enough initial data density in even one city before interest drops off?\n3. What is actual individual willingness to pay, given the entire comparable set is free or investor-funded?\n4. What liability exposure exists for a solo operator from inaccurate safety data?\n5. Is there a narrower, monetizable data/analytics wedge (selling aggregated hazard data to cities/insurers) worth testing instead of the consumer wiki-map framing?\n\n## Regulatory Setup\n- Content moderation / Section 230-type protections: one-time legal consult, ongoing moderation work.\n- Data privacy for location/incident data: one-time privacy review, especially for international users (see BikeMaps.org's government-funded structure). [https://tirf.ca/projects/bikemaps-org-a-tool-for-safe-and-accessible-bicycling/]\n- Liability waiver and insurance: one-time consult before public launch.\n"
